ARIA-1:

An Adaptive Radiation-Immune Quadrant AI Accelerator with Hybrid Spike-Tensor Compute and Thermal-Aware RRAM Drift Management

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: Gemini, Kimi, Deepseek 

Abstract

Deploying artificial intelligence at the orbital edge imposes a fundamental trilemma: no semiconductor architecture simultaneously satisfies real-time inference performance, multi-year radiation tolerance, and the power envelope of nanosatellite platforms. This dissertation presents ARIA-1, a heterogeneous system-on-chip concept that dynamically composes four compute domains to navigate this trade space. The architecture introduces a quadrant-domain SoC with a permanently active radiation-hardened neuromorphic core, two RRAM-based compute-in-memory engines of differing total-ionizing-dose tolerances, and a Hybrid Spike-Tensor Engine that natively executes both spiking neural network inference and integer tensor operations.

This work is positioned as a theoretical and computational framework, grounded in published radiation effects data, predictive process design kit parameters, and physically derived compact models. The coupled drift model for HfO₂ RRAM is reframed as a worst-case analysis for thermally stressing mission phases, with explicit acknowledgment that nominal operating temperatures may not activate significant thermal drift. A thermal profile model spanning sun-pointing operations, eclipse transitions, and radiator degradation scenarios establishes the conditions under which the multiplicative radiation-thermal coupling becomes first-order.

A flux-event classifier pairs on-die PIN diode sensor models augmented by external radiation monitors with a lightweight leaky-integrate-and-fire spiking neural network, achieving sub-millisecond classification latency for galactic cosmic ray transients, South Atlantic Anomaly passages, and solar particle events in simulation. The thermal-aware recalibration protocol with stuck-at fault detection and redundant-row remapping maintains inference accuracy within specification across a simulated ten-year mission, with cadence selection explicitly bounded by RRAM endurance limits and energy budgets.

The domain controller is formalized as a periodic non-homogeneous continuous-time Markov chain with simulation-derived protective-state occupancy, replacing the inadequate two-state renewal approximation with a numerical validation approach. Projected effective throughput ranges from 11.8 TOPS during solar maximum to 14.2 TOPS during solar minimum at 2.88–3.30 TOPS/W mean efficiency, with five-year system reliability of $0.83 \pm 0.10$ (95% confidence interval) under correlated failure assumptions. The dissertation concludes with a revised MPW-realistic fabrication roadmap establishing a credible path from theoretical framework to physical implementation.

