Chapter 7: Dual-CIM Subsystem

7.1 Crossbar Architecture

Domains B and C provide the primary compute-in-memory capability for dense matrix-vector multiplication. Each crossbar tile comprises 256 rows and 256 columns of HfO₂ RRAM cells, with 10% redundancy rows and columns for stuck-at fault remapping.

The tiles operate in current-mode: input digital-to-analog converters drive the word lines with voltages representing input activations, and output analog-to-digital converters sense the bit line currents. For an INT4 tensor operation, each cell stores a 2-bit weight (four conductance levels), and each input is represented by a 4-bit voltage level. The output current is digitized by an 8-bit ADC and accumulated across tiles.

7.2 Domain B: High-Performance CIM

Domain B comprises 128 crossbar tiles fabricated in 22 nm FD-SOI, providing 8.4 million RRAM cells. At peak throughput, the domain executes 8.5 TOPS of INT4 operations with an analog-to-digital conversion overhead of approximately 18%. The total ionizing dose tolerance of 80–120 krad limits continuous operation in high-flux environments, motivating the graduated activation hierarchy.

The 22 nm FD-SOI process provides higher logic density than the 28 nm radiation-hardened process, enabling more crossbar tiles per unit area. However, the lack of radiation-hardening-by-design features results in lower total ionizing dose tolerance. The RRAM cells themselves are inherently single-event upset hard due to the filamentary switching mechanism, but the peripheral CMOS circuits (ADCs, DACs, decoders) remain susceptible to single-event functional interrupts.

7.3 Domain C: Radiation-Tolerant CIM

Domain C comprises 48 crossbar tiles fabricated in 28 nm FD-SOI with radiation-hardening-by-design, providing 3.1 million RRAM cells. The radiation-hardened process reduces crossbar cell density by approximately 40% compared to Domain B, but increases total ionizing dose tolerance to 200 krad. Domain C serves as the intermediate-performance option during elevated flux periods when Domain B is gated.

7.4 Peripheral Hardening

While the RRAM cells themselves are inherently single-event upset hard, the peripheral CMOS circuits remain susceptible to single-event functional interrupts. The ARIA-1 design pairs radiation-hardened peripheral state machines in 28 nm FD-SOI with the analog crossbar core. A single-event functional interrupt in a peripheral circuit triggers a tile-level checkpoint recovery rather than a domain-wide reset.

The checkpoint recovery mechanism works as follows. Each tile maintains a shadow copy of its configuration state (DAC/ADC settings, remapping table) in radiation-hardened registers. On detection of a single-event functional interrupt, the tile controller restores the configuration from the shadow copy and re-executes the interrupted operation. This reduces the recovery time from milliseconds to microseconds, minimizing the throughput impact of single-event functional interrupts.

