Chapter 8: Hybrid Spike-Tensor Engine

8.1 Dual-Mode Tile Microarchitecture

Each HSTE tile comprises a 256×256 RRAM crossbar with configurable peripherals. In spike mode, the digital-to-analog converters operate in a binary pulse mode, generating fixed-amplitude voltage pulses for input spikes. The output current is integrated over a 10 ns window and compared to a threshold, implementing the synaptic dot product of an SNN layer.

In tensor mode, the digital-to-analog converters generate variable-amplitude voltages representing INT4 activations, and the output currents are digitized by 8-bit analog-to-digital converters.

8.2 Mode-Switching Latency and Energy

While digital control registers can be updated within a single clock cycle of 5 ns, the analog reference circuits exhibit capacitive settling delays. To prevent read-disturb transients on programmed RRAM states, the design incorporates a settling margin of 10 clock cycles during mode transitions, corresponding to 50 ns at the 200 MHz operating frequency.

Energy comparison with external accelerator attachment: On-chip mode switching consumes approximately $P_D \times t_{\text{switch}} = 1.2 \text{ W} \times 50 \text{ ns} = 60$ nJ per switch. In contrast, moving a 2 MB intermediate activation tensor from Domain B to an external accelerator via PCIe Gen 3 costs approximately 2 MB × 50 pJ/bit = 800 μJ. The ratio is over four orders of magnitude in favor of on-chip mode switching.

For workloads that alternate modes at layer granularity, the cumulative settling overhead is approximately 2.4% of the total execution time for a typical hybrid network with 12 alternating layers. This overhead is accounted for in the effective throughput calculation.

The settling margin can be reduced through a dual-bank architecture that pre-charges the alternate mode's reference rail prior to switching. In this approach, each tile maintains two sets of reference circuits, one for each mode, with the inactive set continuously pre-charged to the correct voltage. Mode switching then requires only a multiplexer selection, reducing the settling time to approximately 2 clock cycles. This architecture is under investigation as future work.

8.3 Optimal Hybrid Split

The throughput-optimal fraction of tensor-mode tiles was derived in Proposition 4.1. For the representative workload with $\rho_{\text{dense}} = 0.8$ and $\rho_{\text{sparse}} = 0.15$, the optimal split is $\beta^* = 0.731$, yielding an effective throughput of 4.25 TOPS.

8.4 Wear-Leveling for RRAM Endurance

The recalibration protocol subjects RRAM cells to repeated write-verify cycles. Over a ten-year mission, the average write cycle count per cell reaches approximately $3.2 \times 10^4$, well below the endurance limit of $1 \times 10^5$ for analog multi-level HfO₂ RRAM. However, write-heavy cells may approach the limit earlier, motivating wear-leveling.

To mitigate this, the HSTE domain implements probabilistic wear-leveling within the crossbar redundancy rows. When a tile undergoes recalibration, physical row assignments are rotated so that write-heavy parameters are periodically mapped to less-frequently updated spare rows. The rotation is governed by a pseudo-random sequence seeded by the tile ID and recalibration cycle count, ensuring uniform wear distribution across the redundancy rows.

The wear-leveling algorithm maintains a write counter for each physical row, stored in the defect map. On each recalibration, the rows with the highest write counts are rotated to the spare rows with the lowest write counts. This extends the effective endurance by approximately 3×, from $1 \times 10^5$ to $3 \times 10^5$ effective write cycles, providing a comfortable margin over the ten-year mission requirement.

