Chapter 9: Thermal-Aware Recalibration Protocol

9.1 MPNV Protocol Overview

The thermal-aware recalibration protocol, designated MPNV, maintains RRAM weight accuracy through periodic write-verify cycles. The protocol comprises four phases: conductance measurement, stuck-at fault detection, corrective programming, and remapping table update.

The protocol operates in three thermal regimes:

· Regime I (Thermally frozen): Cadence determined by TID drift only. Thermal drift is negligible.
· Regime II (Transitional): Cadence determined by additive TID + thermal drift.
· Regime III (Thermally active): Cadence determined by full coupled model with multiplicative interaction.

The regime is selected based on real-time junction temperature measurements from on-die PTAT thermal sensors.

9.2 Coupled Drift-Aware Cadence Selection

The recalibration interval is determined by the regime-appropriate drift model. For Regime III, the instantaneous joint drift rate is

\frac{d\Delta G}{dt} = G(0) \frac{dD}{dt} \left(1 + \kappa\,\epsilon_T(T)\right),

where the term $(1 + \kappa\epsilon_T(T))$ captures the multiplicative amplification of the total ionizing dose drift by the thermal component.

The recalibration interval is selected to ensure that the expected maximum conductance deviation between recalibrations remains below a specified tolerance $\Delta G_{\text{tol}}$:

\tau_{\text{cal}} = \frac{\Delta G_{\text{tol}}}{G(0) \dot{D}_{\text{eff}} (1 + \kappa\bar\epsilon_T)}.

For Regime I (nominal operations): $\bar\epsilon_T \approx 0$, and the interval reduces to

\tau_{\text{cal,I}} = \frac{\Delta G_{\text{tol}}}{G(0) \dot{D}_{\text{eff}}}.

For the ARIA-1 reference orbit with $\dot{D}_{\text{eff}} = 0.1$ rad/day and $\Delta G_{\text{tol}}/G(0) = 0.02$:

\tau_{\text{cal,I}} = \frac{0.02}{0.1} = 0.2 \text{ days} = 4.8 \text{ hours}.

For Regime III (sun-pointing): $\bar\epsilon_T = 0.10$, $\kappa = 0.38$:

\tau_{\text{cal,III}} = \frac{0.02}{0.1 \times (1 + 0.38 \times 0.10)} = \frac{0.02}{0.1 \times 1.038} = 0.193 \text{ days} = 4.6 \text{ hours}.

The difference between Regime I and Regime III cadences is approximately 4%, confirming that the coupling is a first-order but modest effect even in the thermally active regime. During elevated flux periods (solar particle events), $\dot{D}_{\text{eff}}$ increases to 1 rad/day, reducing the interval to approximately 28 minutes regardless of thermal regime.

9.3 Endurance and Energy Constraints

The recalibration cadence is constrained by RRAM endurance and energy budgets. For a ten-year mission with an average recalibration interval of 4.6–4.8 hours, the total number of recalibrations is approximately $10 \times 365 \times 24 / 4.7 \approx 18,600$. Each recalibration programs only cells whose conductance deviation exceeds the tolerance, which is typically 5–10% of the total cell population. For Domain B with 8.4 million cells, this is approximately 420,000–840,000 cells per recalibration.

The per-cell write energy for HfO₂ RRAM is approximately 1–10 pJ. The energy per recalibration is therefore approximately 0.42–8.4 mJ. At a recalibration interval of 4.6 hours, the average recalibration power is approximately 0.025–0.51 μW, which is negligible compared to the domain's 2.3 W power budget.

The total number of writes per cell over the mission is approximately $18,600 \times 0.05 \approx 930$ writes, well below the endurance limit of $1 \times 10^5$. The worst-case cells (those with the highest drift) may experience up to $18,600 \times 0.1 \approx 1,860$ writes, still well within endurance limits.

9.4 Stuck-At Fault Detection and Remapping

Stuck-at faults are detected during the conductance measurement phase through a three-step check. The cell is programmed to a target conductance, the conductance is measured, and a write pulse is applied. If the conductance does not change in response to the write pulse, the cell is classified as stuck-at.

Detected faults are added to a defect map stored in Domain A SRAM. The logical-to-physical remapping is updated to substitute a spare row or column for the faulty cell.

Observation 9.1 (Remapping Error Preservation). If the spare rows and columns in a crossbar tile are populated with cells from the same conductance distribution as the active cells and programmed using the identical write-verify procedure, the root-mean-square error of the logical matrix-vector product after stuck-at-fault remapping is bounded by the same constant as in a defect-free tile.

Justification. The remapping is a permutation of rows and columns such that the physical conductance matrix is a permuted version of the logical matrix with stuck-at cells replaced by freshly programmed spares. Because the spare cells undergo the identical programming process, their final conductance errors are drawn from the same distribution as the errors of healthy cells after the same number of write-verify cycles. The set of error entries that contribute to any logical dot product is a subset of the full error matrix, with each entry statistically identical to the baseline distribution. The mean-squared error of the dot product is therefore bounded by the same constant as in a defect-free tile. This is a direct consequence of the exchangeability of the error distribution under the permutation, not a separate theorem. ∎

