Chapter 11: System Evaluation and Hypothesis Validation

11.1 Multi-Resolution Simulation Methodology

The simulation framework employs a multi-resolution approach to make ten-year mission simulations computationally tractable. The framework comprises four abstraction layers:

Layer 1: Device-level Monte Carlo. RRAM conductance variability, drift, and stuck-at fault generation are simulated using a Monte Carlo engine with per-cell state tracking. This layer is invoked at recalibration events and at configurable checkpoints, not at every clock cycle.

Layer 2: Circuit-level compact models. Power, timing, and thermal estimates are derived from pre-computed lookup tables generated from SPICE simulations of crossbar tiles, ADCs, and DACs. These tables are embedded in the architectural simulator.

Layer 3: Architectural event-driven simulation. The four domains, NoC, and domain controller are simulated using an event-driven framework (SystemC) at transaction granularity. Neural network layer executions are modeled as events with known latency and power from the compact model lookup tables.

Layer 4: System-level Markov chain. The domain controller state transitions are analyzed using the periodic non-homogeneous CTMC formulation, with transition rates derived from the flux process and classifier behavior.

The ten-year mission is simulated by compressing periods of nominal operation into event-driven transactions. Radiation events (SEPs, SAA passages) are injected as high-resolution time segments, while quiet periods are simulated at coarser granularity. The simulation wall-clock time for one ten-year run is approximately 8 hours on a standard workstation.

11.2 Workload Suite

The workload suite comprises three neural network workloads representative of orbital edge AI applications:

1. Hyperspectral anomaly detector (7-layer CNN+SNN hybrid, 2.3 million parameters)
2. SAR ship classifier (ResNet-18 backbone, 11.2 million parameters)
3. Temporal anomaly SNN (3-layer LIF, 145,000 parameters)

Each workload is compiled for the ARIA-1 architecture using the compilation framework of Chapter 10.

11.3 Baseline Architectures

Hypothesis 1 requires rigorous baselines. Five baseline architectures are defined:

Baseline 1: Dual-domain. A two-domain architecture comprising a radiation-hardened neuromorphic core (equivalent to Domain A) and a single CIM engine (equivalent to Domain B). This represents the state of the art in hybrid radiation-hardened AI accelerators.

Baseline 2: Homogeneous CIM. A single-domain architecture comprising six CIM engines equivalent to Domain B, without radiation-hardened backup. This represents a performance-optimized but radiation-vulnerable design.

Baseline 3: Homogeneous neuromorphic. A single-domain architecture comprising eight neuromorphic cores equivalent to Domain A. This represents a radiation-hardened but performance-limited design.

Baseline 4: COTS accelerator with TMR. A commercial off-the-shelf edge AI accelerator (equivalent to a known 7 TOPS INT8 accelerator) with triple-modular redundancy for radiation mitigation. This represents the conventional approach to space-grade AI.

Baseline 5: NASA HPSC-like vector processor. A homogeneous vector processor architecture modeled on the PIC64-HPSC specification: eight RISC-V vector cores with AI/ML extensions, 2 TOPS INT8, 40 W, with dual-core lockstep and EDAC/ECC. This represents the current state of the art in space-grade AI acceleration.

All baselines are subject to the same radiation traces, fault injection, and accuracy constraints as ARIA-1. The baselines are implemented in the same simulation framework.

11.4 Fault Injection Campaign

A systematic fault injection campaign is conducted to validate reliability claims. The campaign injects three fault types:

Bit flips in SRAM (SEU). Injected at rates derived from CREME96 models for the target orbit, scaled by per-domain SEU cross-sections from published 28nm and 22nm FD-SOI data.

Stuck-at faults in RRAM. Injected at rates derived from accelerated aging models, with both hard stuck-at faults (permanent) and soft stuck-at faults (recoverable via reprogramming).

Single-event functional interrupts. Injected at rates derived from heavy-ion test data on similar process nodes, targeting ADCs, DACs, and state machines.

The campaign injects 250,000 faults per ten-year run across 200 independent runs, totaling 50 million injected faults per architecture. This increase from the previous 50 runs is motivated by the need to tighten statistical confidence intervals on reliability estimates.

11.5 Hypothesis 1: Quadrant Dominance

ARIA-1 is compared against the five baselines under identical radiation traces, fault injection, and accuracy constraints. Table 11.1 summarizes the results.

Architecture Effective TOPS Mean Power (W) TOPS/W 10-Year Accuracy Drop (%)
ARIA-1 11.8–14.2 4.1–4.3 2.88–3.30 0.42
Dual-domain 7.2 3.8 1.89 0.51
Homogeneous CIM 15.1 6.8 2.22 4.2 (fails)
Homogeneous neuromorphic 2.2 1.5 1.47 0.15
COTS + TMR 5.6 9.4 0.60 0.38
HPSC-like vector 2.0 40.0 0.05 0.25

ARIA-1 achieves a 64% increase in effective TOPS over the dual-domain baseline at a 13% power increase, with a comparable accuracy drop. Relative to the HPSC-like baseline, ARIA-1 achieves 5.9× higher effective throughput at 9.8× lower power, yielding a 57.6× energy efficiency advantage. This efficiency gap arises from three factors: analog CIM (10–20× per-MAC energy reduction), native neuromorphic execution (10–50× for SNN workloads), and dynamic domain adaptation (recovers throughput during benign periods).

The homogeneous CIM baseline achieves higher raw throughput but fails the accuracy requirement due to radiation-induced drift. The COTS + TMR baseline has poor energy efficiency due to TMR overhead. These results support Hypothesis 1, with the caveat that the absolute throughput figures carry uncertainty from the simulation methodology.

Caveat on the HPSC comparison: The HPSC-like baseline is modeled with a 40 W power budget, reflecting the space-qualified variant of PIC64-HPSC. However, the HPSC's 2 TOPS figure is for INT8 operations with full software ecosystem support. The ARIA-1 comparison assumes INT4 CIM operations, which provide 2× higher throughput per MAC. The effective TOPS figures are not directly comparable without adjusting for precision. A fairer comparison would normalize by equivalent INT8 TOPS, which would reduce ARIA-1's effective throughput to approximately 5.9–7.1 INT8-equivalent TOPS. This is still 3–3.5× higher than HPSC at 10× lower power.

11.6 Hypothesis 2: Coupled Drift Model Fidelity

The thermal-radiation coupled drift model is evaluated by comparing simulated conductance distributions after ten years against predictions of the additive-only model, with explicit separation of thermal regimes.

Regime I (nominal operations): The coupled model and additive model produce statistically indistinguishable results, confirming that the coupling is negligible when thermal drift is frozen.

Regime III (thermally stressing phases): With the coupled drift model and MPNV adaptive cadence, the normalized mean-squared error remains within specification for the entire ten-year period. The additive model underestimates the drift variance, allowing the error to exceed the accuracy threshold by year eight during prolonged Regime III operation.

The variance magnification factor at Regime III thermal conditions is evaluated with $\kappa = 0.38$ and the component variances specified in Chapter 3. The magnification factor is $1.115 \pm 0.004$, consistent with the analytical derivation. The sensitivity to $\kappa$ is shown in Figure 11.1, spanning $\kappa = 0.30$ to $\kappa = 0.60$. The magnification factor ranges from 1.09 to 1.17.

The recalibration cadence is verified to respect endurance and energy constraints throughout the mission. The maximum number of writes per cell is approximately 1,860, well below the endurance limit of $1 \times 10^5$.

11.7 Hypothesis 3: SNN Flux Classifier Accuracy

The classifier is evaluated on a three-year held-out test set. The confusion matrix is:

 Predicted GCR Predicted SAA Predicted SEP
Actual GCR 94.2% 3.1% 2.7%
Actual SAA 1.8% 96.4% 1.8%
Actual SEP 2.2% 1.3% 96.5%

The composite false-positive rate for FORTRESS-triggering events (SEP predictions that are actually GCR or SAA) is 1.8%. The recall for true SEP events is 96.5%. The precision for SEP predictions is 95.6%. The class imbalance in the training data is severe (SEP events are rare), but the classifier maintains high recall through class-weighted training loss.

The throughput recovered by avoiding unnecessary gating is 2.9% of effective TOPS. The SNN power overhead is 11.7 mW, within the 15 mW budget. These results support Hypothesis 3, with the false-positive rate at 1.8% exceeding the original target of 0.8% but still below the revised target of 2%.

11.8 System Reliability

The five-year system reliability is computed using a correlated failure model with a Gaussian copula. With 200 independent Monte Carlo runs, the resulting five-year reliability is $0.83 \pm 0.06$ (95% confidence interval), yielding a confidence interval of $[0.77, 0.89]$. This is a significant tightening from the previous $[0.73, 0.93]$ interval. The system availability is 0.9982, corresponding to 158 hours of downtime over a ten-year mission.

The FORTRESS occupancy observed in simulation is $0.022 \pm 0.003$ (95% confidence interval with 200 runs). This is used directly for reliability calculations, as the two-state analytical approximation is acknowledged to be inadequate (Chapter 4.6).

11.9 Discussion of Approximation Limits

The two-state renewal approximation for FORTRESS occupancy is explicitly acknowledged as inadequate. The approximation ignores FORTRESS entries from extended SAA passages and rare extreme GCR events, which contribute significantly to the observed occupancy. The simulation result of 0.022 should be used for reliability calculations. A corrected analytical bound remains an area for future work.

The deterministic approximation for time-to-accuracy-threshold (Approximation 3.2) introduces errors on the order of 6% due to drift rate fluctuations. This is acceptable for cadence selection but should not be used for high-precision reliability predictions.

The Lyapunov stability conditions for the SNN networks are verified through linear stability analysis and empirical fault injection, but a rigorous proof of global stability for the threshold nonlinearity remains an open question.

The thermal model uses coarse approximations for inter-die coupling and localized heating. The junction temperatures in Appendix E are average values; localized hot spots within the crossbar tiles may exceed these averages by 20–40°C. This uncertainty affects the thermal regime classification and should be resolved through thermal imaging during silicon validation.

