Chapter 12: Revised Fabrication Roadmap and Silicon Validation Protocol

12.1 Transitioning from Theoretical Models to Physical Verification

This chapter establishes a structured bridge between the analytical and computational framework developed in Chapters 3–11 and physical silicon implementation. The protocol has been revised to be MPW-realistic, splitting the test chip into independent single-die runs without requiring multi-die interposer assembly.

12.2 Target Fabrication Platform

The target fabrication platform is a standard Multi-Project Wafer (MPW) shuttle run on:

· 28 nm FD-SOI (e.g., STMicroelectronics 28FDSOI) for Domains A and C
· 22 nm FD-SOI (e.g., GlobalFoundries 22FDX) for Domains B and D

Each die is fabricated on a separate MPW run and packaged independently in a ceramic quad flat pack (CQFP). System-level integration is achieved through PCB-level connections, not interposer assembly. This approach is compatible with standard university MPW access (EUROPRACTICE, MOSIS) and avoids the significant infrastructure requirements of silicon interposer fabrication.

12.3 Test-Chip Architecture and Floorplanning

The test chip comprises scaled-down versions of each domain, sufficient for functional and radiation characterization:

· Domain A mini: 64 neuromorphic cores (6% of full scale) on 28 nm FD-SOI
· Domain B mini: 8 crossbar tiles (6% of full scale) on 22 nm FD-SOI
· Domain C mini: 4 crossbar tiles (8% of full scale) on 28 nm FD-SOI
· Domain D mini: 4 dual-mode tiles (8% of full scale) on 22 nm FD-SOI
· BIST and diagnostic infrastructure: Boundary scan chains, ring oscillators, PTAT thermal sensors
· External sensor interface: Connector for commercial radiation monitor (RADMON, Liulin) input

The on-die PIN diode array is retained as a research curiosity for SET pulse-width characterization, but is explicitly not the primary flux sensor. The external radiation monitor provides the flux input for SNN classifier validation.

12.4 Total Ionizing Dose Exposure Protocol

TID testing follows MIL-STD-883 Method 1019.9 and ESCC Basic Specification No. 22900 using a Cobalt-60 gamma source or 10 keV X-ray tool. Dose rates:

· High Dose Rate (HDR): 50–300 rad(Si)/s to evaluate maximum TID failure limits up to 300 krad(Si)
· Low Dose Rate (LDR): 0.01–0.1 rad(Si)/s to assess Enhanced Low Dose Rate Sensitivity (ELDRS)

Parameters monitored include RRAM conductance drift $\Delta G_{ij}(t)$, threshold voltage shifts, and subthreshold leakage. The empirical thermal-radiation coupling coefficient $\kappa_{\text{emp}}$ is extracted from measured drift curves at controlled temperatures spanning 25–125°C. This directly tests the thermal regime applicability criteria of Chapter 3.

12.5 Single-Event Effects Heavy-Ion Beam Test Protocol

Heavy-ion testing is conducted at facilities such as LBNL 88-Inch Cyclotron, GANIL, or CERN CHARM. The test matrix spans LET values from 1 to 85 MeV·cm²/mg using ion species including Ne, Ar, Kr, and Xe at incidence angles from 0° to 60°.

Cross-section data are fitted to the Weibull model:

\sigma(\text{LET}_{\text{eff}}) = \begin{cases} 0, & \text{for } \text{LET}_{\text{eff}} < \text{LET}_{\text{th}} \\ \sigma_{\text{sat}} \left[ 1 - \exp\left( -\left( \frac{\text{LET}_{\text{eff}} - \text{LET}_{\text{th}}}{W} \right)^S \right) \right], & \text{for } \text{LET}_{\text{eff}} \ge \text{LET}_{\text{th}} \end{cases}

with $\sigma_{\text{sat}}$, $\text{LET}_{\text{th}}$, $W$, and $S$ extracted via Maximum Likelihood Estimation.

12.6 Closed-Loop Model Calibration

Empirical parameters from silicon testing are fed back into the simulation framework to refine the analytical models:

· Measured SEU cross-sections update the fault injection layer
· Measured $\kappa_{\text{emp}}$ updates the drift model and validates the thermal regime classification
· Measured gating latency updates the CTMC transition rates
· Empirical FORTRESS occupancy validates the Monte Carlo simulation approach and provides a benchmark for future analytical bounds

The calibrated reliability predictions are used to refine design-for-reliability guidelines for a full-mask production tape-out.

