Appendix A: Proofs and Derivations for Chapter 3 (Coupled Drift Model)

A.1 Statement of the Coupled Drift Model

The conductance of cell $(i,j)$ at time $t$ is modeled as

G_{ij}(t) = G_{ij}(0)\left[1 + \epsilon_{\text{TID}}(D(t)) + \epsilon_T(T(t)) + \kappa\,\epsilon_{\text{TID}}(D(t))\,\epsilon_T(T(t))\right] + \xi_{ij}(t),

where:

· $G_{ij}(0)$ is the initial programmed conductance,
· $\epsilon_{\text{TID}}(D)$ is the fractional conductance change due to total ionizing dose $D$,
· $\epsilon_T(T)$ is the fractional conductance change due to temperature $T$,
· $\kappa$ is the coupling coefficient (uncertain parameter, nominal 0.38, range 0.30–0.60),
· $\xi_{ij}(t)$ is a zero-mean noise process with variance $\sigma_\xi^2$.

Critical epistemic status: This model is a theoretical construct derived from published device physics. No empirical validation has been performed on the target RRAM stack. The model is presented as a working hypothesis whose parameters require experimental determination through the silicon validation protocol of Chapter 12.

A.2 Thermal Regime Applicability Criteria

The thermal drift term follows an Arrhenius law:

\epsilon_T(T) = \epsilon_{T,0}\,\exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{k_B T}\right),

with activation energy $E_a \approx 0.84$ eV for HfO₂, $k_B = 8.617 \times 10^{-5}$ eV/K, and $T$ in Kelvin.

Numerical evaluation of the Arrhenius term:

