C.4 External Sensor Characterization

The external radiation monitor is modeled as a silicon detector with the following specifications:

Parameter Value
Detector area 1 cm²
Depletion depth 300 μm
Threshold energy 10 MeV (protons)
Count rate (quiet) 10–1000 counts/s
Count rate (SEP) Up to 10⁵ counts/s
Time resolution 10 μs

Flux estimation from count rate:

\hat\Phi(t) = \frac{N(t, t + \Delta t)}{A \cdot \Delta t \cdot \eta},

where $N(t, t + \Delta t)$ is the number of counts in the interval $[t, t + \Delta t]$, $A$ is the detector area, and $\eta$ is the detection efficiency.

For a 1 ms encoding window ($\Delta t = 1$ ms), the statistical uncertainty in the flux estimate during quiet periods (10 counts/s) is

\frac{\sigma_{\hat\Phi}}{\hat\Phi} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{0.01}} = 10.

This corresponds to a 1000% relative uncertainty during quiet periods, which is unacceptable for precise flux estimation. A longer integration window or a larger detector area is required for quiet-period measurements. During SEP events (10⁵ counts/s), the relative uncertainty is

\frac{\sigma_{\hat\Phi}}{\hat\Phi} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{100}} = 0.1 = 10\%.

This asymmetry suggests that the classifier should rely primarily on coarse flux magnitude during quiet periods and switch to fine-grained derivative analysis during elevated flux events.

