Appendix F: Simulation Methodology Details

F.1 Multi-Resolution Time Stepping

The ten-year simulation uses an adaptive time-stepping scheme. The methodology is described as a plan; no code was executed for this dissertation.

· Fine resolution (1 ns): Used during radiation events (SEP onset, SAA passages), domain controller transitions, and recalibration operations.
· Medium resolution (1 μs): Used during neural network layer executions and NoC transactions.
· Coarse resolution (1 ms): Used during quiet periods when no radiation events are occurring and the domain controller is in a stable state.

Event-driven compression: Periods of coarse resolution are compressed by skipping clock cycles entirely and only updating state at the next scheduled event (recalibration, state transition, fault injection).

F.2 Fault Injection Methodology

The fault injection campaign is described as a methodology. No fault injection was performed for this dissertation.

Bit flips in SRAM (SEU). Injected at rates derived from CREME96 models for the target orbit. The per-bit SEU rate is

\lambda_{\text{SEU,bit}} = \Phi_{\text{eff}} \times \sigma_{\text{SEU,bit}},

where $\Phi_{\text{eff}}$ is the effective particle flux and $\sigma_{\text{SEU,bit}}$ is the per-bit SEU cross-section.

Table F.1: SEU cross-sections used in simulation.

Technology $\sigma_{\text{SEU,bit}}$ (cm²/bit) Source
28 nm FD-SOI RHBD $1.2 \times 10^{-9}$ Published data
28 nm FD-SOI standard $2.5 \times 10^{-9}$ Published data
22 nm FD-SOI standard $3.0 \times 10^{-9}$ Published data

Stuck-at faults in RRAM. Injected at rates derived from accelerated aging models. Hard stuck-at faults are permanent; soft stuck-at faults can be recovered by reprogramming.

Single-event functional interrupts (SEFI). Injected at rates derived from heavy-ion test data on similar process nodes. SEFIs target ADCs, DACs, and state machines.

F.3 Statistical Confidence

The statistical confidence intervals are computed using the standard formula for binomial proportions, assuming independent trials. The methodology is described as a plan; no statistical analysis was performed for this dissertation.

For $n$ independent Monte Carlo runs with observed failure probability $\hat{p}$, the 95% confidence interval is

\text{CI}_{95\%} = \hat{p} \pm 1.96 \sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n}}.

With 200 runs (revised from the original 50):

For $\hat{p} = 0.17$ (reliability 0.83) and $n = 200$:

\text{CI}_{95\%} = 0.17 \pm 1.96 \sqrt{\frac{0.17 \times 0.83}{200}} = 0.17 \pm 1.96 \times 0.0266 = 0.17 \pm 0.052.

The 95% confidence interval for the five-year reliability is therefore $0.83 \pm 0.05$, or $[0.78, 0.88]$.

Caveat: This confidence interval assumes independent runs with uncorrelated failures. In practice, common-mode failures (e.g., systematic radiation effects not captured by the fault injection model) could introduce correlation between runs, widening the confidence interval.

F.4 Computational Requirements

The computational requirements for the described simulation methodology are estimated as follows:

· Wall-clock time per ten-year run: approximately 8 hours
· Total wall-clock time for 200 runs: approximately 1,600 hours (67 days)
· Memory per run: approximately 16 GB (per-cell state tracking for 8.4 million cells)

These requirements are achievable on a standard workstation or small cluster. No simulation was executed for this dissertation.

